The invention relates to a method for transmitting analog and digital information during energy transmission between a power transmitter (10) and a power receiver (12) galvanically isolated from the power transmitter. The method comprises the following steps:

a) providing the analog (22a) and digital (24a) information to be transmitted;  
b) defining the number nAD of possible values ADi, where i = 1 to nAD, which are possible when digitizing the analog information, and defining the value range of the values ADi;  
c) assigning a digital value Dx, in binary notation, to the digital information;  
d) defining a minimum number of events nmin to be transmitted;  
e) determining a maximum number of events nmax to be transmitted;  
f) defining a first set of values nlow according to: nlow(ADi) = nmin + f1(ADi), where i = 1 to nAD;  
g) defining a second set of values nhigh according to: nhigh(ADi) = nmax − f2(ADi), where i = 1 to nAD;  
h) digitizing the analog information to obtain a digital analog value ADx;  
i) determining a number of events nlow(ADx) = nmin + f1(ADx) for the respective bit of the digital value Dx to be transmitted if the current bit of the digital value Dx to be transmitted is 0, and defining a number of events nhigh(ADx) = nmax − f2(ADx) for the respective bit of the digital value Dx to be transmitted if the current bit of the digital value Dx to be transmitted is 1, or vice versa; and  
j) transmitting a signal that correlates at least with the number of events determined in step i).

The invention further relates to a corresponding device for carrying out the method.

outputs.L2:
The invention concerns a process for conveying both analog and digital data during the transfer of energy between a power transmitter (10) and a power receiver (12), the receiver being galvanically separated from the transmitter. The process includes the following operations:

a) making available the analog information (22a) and the digital information (24a) that are to be sent;  
b) specifying the number nAD of possible values ADi, where i = 1 to nAD, that may result from digitizing the analog information, and specifying the value range of the values ADi;  
c) associating the digital information with a digital value Dx, expressed in binary form;  
d) setting a minimum number of events nmin to be transmitted;  
e) establishing a maximum number of events nmax to be transmitted;  
f) creating a first group of values nlow, defined as: nlow(ADi) = nmin + f1(ADi), for i = 1 to nAD;  
g) creating a second group of values nhigh, defined as: nhigh(ADi) = nmax − f2(ADi), for i = 1 to nAD;  
h) converting the analog information into digital form so as to produce a digital analog value ADx;  
i) for the respective bit of the digital value Dx that is to be transmitted, selecting the number of events nlow(ADx) = nmin + f1(ADx) when the current bit to be transmitted is 0, and selecting the number of events nhigh(ADx) = nmax − f2(ADx) when the current bit to be transmitted is 1, or alternatively applying the opposite assignment; and  
j) sending a signal that is correlated at least with the number of events determined in step i).

The invention also covers a corresponding apparatus configured to perform this method.

counterfactuals.output:
The invention relates to a wireless or galvanically isolated power transmitter and receiver system for transferring electrical energy from a transmitter side to a receiver side while simultaneously communicating analog and digital data across the same isolation barrier or wireless coupling path.

In one aspect, the system comprises a power transmitter configured to generate an alternating excitation signal and to apply said excitation signal to a primary coupling element, and a power receiver comprising a secondary coupling element configured to receive energy from the primary coupling element and to supply a load. The coupling elements may comprise inductive coils, capacitive plates, transformer windings, resonant coupling structures, or another contactless or isolated energy-transfer arrangement.

The transmitter and receiver are further configured to exchange information by modulating at least one operating parameter of the energy-transfer signal, wherein the operating parameter may comprise frequency, phase, amplitude, duty cycle, burst length, pulse density, resonant detuning, load modulation, or a combination thereof. The information exchanged includes at least one analog value and at least one digital value, such that power transfer and data transfer take place concurrently or in time-interleaved intervals without requiring a separate galvanic data channel.

In an embodiment, an analog data value ADi is represented by a number n of pulses, cycles, switching events, time slots, or modulation intervals within a predetermined observation window. A minimum number nmin and a maximum number nmax define an allowable coding range. The value ADi may be mapped to n according to a linear or non-linear transfer function, for example such that a lower analog input value corresponds to nmin and a higher analog input value corresponds to nmax. The receiver may determine ADi by counting or otherwise evaluating n during the observation window.

In a further embodiment, digital data Dx is transmitted by selecting one of at least two sub-ranges within the available coding range. A first digital state may be represented by a value n within a lower range defined by nlow, and a second digital state may be represented by a value n within an upper range defined by nhigh. The limits nlow and nhigh may be selected between nmin and nmax so as to provide noise margins between analog and digital coding regions or between different digital states.

In a further aspect, the invention provides a method for operating a wireless or isolated power transmitter and receiver system, the method comprising:

1. generating, at a transmitter, an energy-transfer signal for transferring power to a receiver across a wireless or galvanically isolated coupling path;

2. supplying, at the receiver, electrical energy derived from the energy-transfer signal to a load or an energy-storage element;

3. defining a coding range between a minimum count nmin and a maximum count nmax;

4. receiving or generating an analog data value ADi to be communicated across the coupling path;

5. converting the analog data value ADi into a corresponding count value n within the range from nmin to nmax;

6. modulating the energy-transfer signal in accordance with the count value n, thereby embedding the analog data value ADi in the power-transfer signal;

7. receiving, at the opposite side of the coupling path, the modulated energy-transfer signal and determining the count value n;

8. reconstructing the analog data value ADi from the determined count value n;

9. receiving or generating a digital data value Dx to be communicated across the coupling path;

10. assigning the digital data value Dx to a count range, wherein a first digital state is associated with a lower count region including nlow and a second digital state is associated with an upper count region including nhigh;

11. modulating the energy-transfer signal or a load condition of the receiver in accordance with the assigned count range;

12. detecting, at the opposite side of the coupling path, whether the resulting count value lies in the lower count region or the upper count region; and

13. decoding the detected count region as the digital data value Dx while maintaining transfer of electrical energy.

The values nmin, nmax, nlow, and nhigh may be fixed, programmable, calibrated during start-up, or dynamically adapted according to coupling strength, load condition, noise level, temperature, supply voltage, or required data rate. In particular, nlow and nhigh may be separated by a guard band to improve immunity against jitter, component tolerances, electromagnetic interference, or variations in transferred power.

The system may be bidirectional. In a first communication direction, the transmitter modulates the excitation signal to send ADi and/or Dx to the receiver. In a second communication direction, the receiver modulates its input impedance, rectifier loading, resonant tuning, or energy consumption to send ADi and/or Dx back to the transmitter. The transmitter may detect the receiver-side modulation by monitoring current, voltage, phase shift, reflected impedance, resonance frequency, or power demand at the primary coupling element.

The invention thereby enables power transfer, analog communication, and digital communication over a common wireless or isolated interface, reducing the number of isolation components, improving compactness, and allowing monitored and controlled operation of isolated sensors, actuators, battery-powered devices, medical devices, industrial modules, gate drivers, measurement circuits, or other electrically isolated subsystems.
